        {
          "key": "root",
          "label": "root",
          "type": "any"
        }
      ],
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "# Workspace Semantic Presentation\n\n**Status: proposed experiment**\n\nThe current runtime proves that a graph can replace its internal rendering with a graph-level view. This draft refines when that happens, which authored view appears, and how graph authoring should control the result.\n\nThe proposal is deliberately reviewable rather than normative."
        },
        "interfaceContract": {
          "version": 1,
          "receivesViewContent": true
        },
        "identity": {
          "graphId": "workspace-semantic-presentation-declaration",
          "nodeId": "workspace-semantic-presentation-landing-surface"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-problem",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "What feels wrong",
      "position": {
        "x": -1180,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 340,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## What feels wrong\n\nThe current experiment first selects Detail at the old dot threshold. By then, the projected graph has too few screen pixels for Detail to remain readable. The recursive idea is sound, but the presentation band and rendering path are wrong."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-rule",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Proposed recursive rule",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Proposed recursive rule\n\nA graph participating in a workspace is a node-like projection of its declaration.\n\n- Close: render expanded graph contents.\n- Medium: render the exposed Detail View.\n- Far: render the exposed Summary View.\n- Farther: render the exposed Icon View.\n- Extreme: render the exposed Glyph.\n\nGlyph is terminal. If the renderer has enough room for a dot, it has enough room to preserve authored identity with a glyph."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-selection",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Shared bands, relative viewport later",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Shared bands first\n\nAll graph-nodes in one workspace use the same camera zoom bands, so they respond together. Full internal graph extent must not decide whether a graph changes representation; distant nodes and plumbing would make otherwise equivalent graphs behave differently.\n\nThe later declaration/landing viewport can define a meaningful relative footprint and baseline without coupling presentation to the full document extent. Add hysteresis around the shared boundaries so views do not flicker."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-authoring",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Authoring contract",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": -140
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 400,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## The declaration authors the outside view\n\nThe declaration exposes Detail, Summary, Icon, Glyph, and Landing surfaces. Those are the graph’s authored representations when another workspace treats the graph as a node.\n\nEditing graph contents defines the inside. Editing exposed views defines how the graph appears from outside at each scale. Current new-graph defaults provide a complete publishable fallback."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-rendering",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Rendering contract",
      "position": {
        "x": -1180,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Render the real view\n\nWorkspace presentation should use the ordinary view renderer, including the authored payload, style, dimensions, and delegation rules. A special compact text preview is insufficient.\n\nOnce the level leaves Expanded, hide the graph’s internal node, edge, group, handle, and interface layers and render exactly one exposed representation."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-geometry",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Geometry and interfaces",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Geometry follows the chosen representation\n\nSelection, minimap regions, workspace edges, bridge apertures, portal routes, marquee hit testing, and collision placement use the selected presentation bounds.\n\nPorts, portals, and bridges remain stable workspace interfaces. Semantic presentation changes the graph content projection; it must not erase or silently move its capabilities."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-editing",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Proposed authoring experience",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Authoring experience\n\nThe graph author can preview Expanded, Detail, Summary, Icon, and Glyph without manipulating the whole workspace camera. The declaration editor shows which surface owns each level and whether a fallback is being used.\n\nA newly created graph begins with all standard views. Authors may replace their content or delegate them to graph-owned nodes."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-implementation",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Implementation slices",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": 340
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 390,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Proposed implementation slices\n\n1. Replace the collapsed boolean with an explicit workspace presentation level.\n2. Resolve all graph-nodes through shared semantic zoom bands.\n3. Make Glyph terminal and promote the focused graph one level.\n4. Render the actual exposed Detail, Summary, Icon, and Glyph nodes through the normal renderer.\n5. Route workspace geometry through presentation bounds.\n6. Add hysteresis and declaration-level preview controls.\n7. Use the declaration/landing viewport as the later relative spatial baseline."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-tests",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Reviewable smoke contract",
      "position": {
        "x": -600,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Smoke contract\n\nOpen several current-standard graphs with distinct Detail, Summary, Icon, and Glyph content. Zoom across each boundary in both directions. Verify:\n\n- the right authored view appears at each band;\n- no internal dot field remains beneath a graph-level view;\n- the focused graph participates;\n- interfaces and typed workspace edges remain connected;\n- semantic zoom Off keeps graphs expanded;\n- transitions do not flicker."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-questions",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Questions for review",
      "position": {
        "x": 20,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Questions for review\n\n- Which minimum screen dimensions make Detail, Summary, and Icon useful?\n- Should authors eventually set only a relative semantic baseline?\n- Should focus promotion change layout bounds or render within a reserved footprint?\n- When does the declaration/landing viewport replace full graph extent as the workspace footprint?"
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-checkpoint",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Review checkpoint",
      "position": {
        "x": 640,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 560,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Review checkpoint\n\nWorking decisions:\n\n1. Graph-level semantic presentation is recursive view selection.\n2. Graph-nodes respond together to shared workspace zoom bands.\n3. Exposed declaration views are authoritative and render as themselves.\n4. Glyph is the terminal representation; graphs do not become anonymous dots.\n5. Focus, rather than selection, temporarily promotes one semantic level.\n6. Workspace interfaces survive every visual level.\n7. A later declaration/landing viewport may define relative spatial bounds."
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "workspace-semantic-presentation-viewport",
      "type": "content",
      "label": "Next slice · authored viewport",
      "position": {
        "x": -1180,
        "y": 810
      },
      "width": 520,
      "height": 410,
      "visible": true,
      "showLabel": true,
      "data": {
        "content": {
          "kind": "markdown",
          "value": "## Next slice · authored viewport\n\nThe containing workspace may not need the full internal graph extent. A declaration and its landing surface can define an authored viewport: the portion of the graph presented as the workspace node.\n\nThis could preserve a meaningful composition, reuse the viewport widget, and prevent distant internal plumbing from inflating the parent layout. Keep it separate from the first semantic-level implementation so we can test the presentation model before changing spatial ownership."
        }
      }
    }
